    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/tenure-track-assistant-professor-of-advanced-practice-nursing": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Advanced Practice Nursing",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Advanced Practice Nursing - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Public Health - Clinical Public Health - Faculty: Health Sciences Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Advanced Practice Nursing Deadline 28 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Public Health - Clinical Public Health Bartholins Allé 2 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 171076 Apply The Department of Public Health at Faculty of Health Sciences at Aarhus University invites applications for a position as tenure track Assistant Professor in the field of Advanced Practice Nursing as per 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is a fixed-term full-time position with the possibility of subsequent permanent employment as an associate professor. About the Department The Department of Public Health is a research-intensive environment dedicated to advancing both fundamental scientific knowledge and applied solutions to major public health challenges. Our research spans theoretical, basic, and applied sciences, with the shared ambition of generating impactful knowledge and improving population health. Academic excellence is at the core of everything we do. We are committed to conducting rigorous and innovative research and delivering high-quality teaching. The department is responsible for teaching within public health, sport science, nursing science, health science and related methodological disciplines, as well as a substantial part of the teaching in the medical degree programme. While firmly rooted in our respective disciplines, we actively foster interdisciplinary collaboration to advance innovation and excellence in both research and education. We are equally committed to cultivating a healthy, inclusive, and supportive workplace culture characterized by open dialogue, mutual respect, and an informal atmosphere. We believe that academic excellence and personal well-being are mutually reinforcing and essential to long-term success. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Your job responsibilities We offer an attractive career opportunity for promising and talented researchers from around the world. The position is a career development position with the possibility of transition to a tenured associate professorship. We will work together with you to develop your research talent through supervision, professional networks and pedagogical upgrading. As Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Advanced Practice Nursing your primary tasks will be research and research-based teaching. You will teach and supervise students at Bachelor’s and Master’s level, and you will contribute to the development of the department through research of the highest international quality. In your daily work, you will work closely with both Danish and international colleagues. We are looking for a candidate with a strong clinical background and substantial experience from practice, who is motivated by interdisciplinary collaboration. While this is an early-career academic position, we expect the successful candidate to demonstrate the ability and ambition to assume responsibility for coordinating research activities, fostering collaborations, and contributing proactively to the strategic development of the section. Your main tasks will consist of: Independent research of the highest international quality, including publication. Teaching, guidance and examination of Master's degree students, as well as possible supervision of PhD students. Contributing to interdisciplinary research and collaboration across professions, organisations and sectors. Some degree of contribution towards attracting external research funding. Taking an active role in coordinating and developing the section. Supporting the continued development of a strong, collaborative and internationally oriented research environment. You will report to the Head of Department. Your competences You have independent research qualifications at a high international level and are a clear communicator with experience in teaching and supervision at Master's degree level. We value pedagogical training in supervision or teaching, as well as experience with external research collaborations. Importantly, you bring substantial clinical experience and you understand the value of integrating clinical insight with research and education, and you are committed to strengthening links between research, practice, and policy. We expect you to be able to teach and supervise Master's students in making comprehensive theoretical assessments of citizens with complex healthcare needs within a nursing context. The purpose of these assessments is to identify and propose solutions through interdisciplinary collaboration and coordination across professional and sectoral boundaries. We are particularly interested in candidates who thrive in interdisciplinary environments, recognize the value of diverse professional perspectives, and are able to build productive collaborations across disciplines. Furthermore, although this is a career development position, we expect you to show the maturity, initiative and organisational skills required to take responsibility for coordinating research activities and contributing to the ongoing development and direction of the research section. As a person, you have excellent interpersonal skills, are inclusive and team-oriented, and contribute positively to a supportive and ambitious working environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. International applicants are expected to learn Danish, and Aarhus University offers courses in Danish language. In order to be assessed as qualified for a Tenure Track Assistant Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria . Shortlisting will be used. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of Department, Michael Baggesen Klitgaard at tel.: (+45) 4022 7649. Your place of work will be the Department of Public Health, Bartholins Allé 2, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. The process The tenure track position starts out with appointment as assistant professor with a view to permanent employment as an associated professor. Permanent tenure as an associate professor is conditional upon you being assessed as qualified as such no later than 6 months before the expected transition to associate professor at the latest. In order to be assessed as qualified for an associate professor position, you must meet these academic criteria . You will be appointed a supervisor by Head of Department and evaluation meetings will take place at least every half year. Required qualifications - Assessment as assistant professor Appointment as assistant professor requires academic qualifications at PhD level. For an applicant to qualify for a tenure track position, these academic criteria must be met in addition to the requirements described in the job advertisement. Terms of employment Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . The yearly base salary for a fulltime assistant professor is between DKK 493.394,35 and DKK 585.322,48 depending on the years of working experience after achieved MSc degree. The base salary includes a position related supplement and pension (18.07 %). Additional supplement(s) for special qualifications can be negotiated. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Travel and moving expenses may be covered according to the employee in question and, to a limited extent, the accompanying family. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial Order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - assistant professor or tenure track assistant professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios A maximum of five of the publications of greatest relevance to the job must be submitted Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - assistant professor or tenure track assistant professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also has a Junior Researcher Development Programme. You can read more about it here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Michael Baggesen Klitgaard Head of Department, Department of Public Health +4540227649 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4520874354 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Wednesday 28 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/student-assistant-within-cognitive-neuroscience-and-computational-psychiatry": {
      "title": "Student Assistant within Cognitive Neuroscience and Computational Psychiatry",
      "description": "Student Assistant within Cognitive Neuroscience and Computational Psychiatry - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Clinical Medicine - Center of Functionally Integrative Neuroscience Student Assistant within Cognitive Neuroscience and Computational Psychiatry Deadline 29 Oct 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Jan Department of Clinical Medicine - Center of Functionally Integrative Neuroscience Universitetsbyen 3 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term part-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Aug 2028 10 hours per week ID: 171229 Apply The Department of Clinical Medicine, Faculty of Health, Aarhus University invites applications for a position as a Student Assistant in the field of Cognitive Neuroscience and Computational Psychiatry. The position is for the period 1 January 2027 (or as soon as possible thereafter) to 31 August 2028. The position is a fixed-term, part-time position, for 10 hours per week in the first instance. As a Student Assistant at the Department of Clinical Medicine, you will be part of one of the largest health science research departments in Denmark. Our clinical research covers all medical specialities and takes place in close collaboration with Aarhus University Hospital and the regional hospitals in the Central Denmark Region. We are around 670 academic employees, 500 PhD students and 160 technical/administrative employees who are cooperating across disciplines. You will be working within the Center for Functionally Integrative Neuroscience (CFIN). You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . CFIN is an international, multidisciplinary research centre. Research at CFIN include fields such as cognitive neuroscience, neuroimaging, machine learning and biophysics. Investigators at CFIN are supported by state-of-the-art research facilities, including MRI, MEG, OPM-MEG, EEG, PET, TMS, eye-tracking and more. CFIN is part of the Danish Neuroscience Center (DNC) and the NeuroCampus Aarhus network alongside other leading neuroscience centres such as DANDRITE. You can read more about CFIN here . About the position The successful applicant will join the Deep Cognitive Neuroscience (DCN) lab, led by Dan Bang, at CFIN. The DCN lab applies a multi-modal, cognitive neuroscience-based approach to understanding decision-making, learning and mental representations. The DNC lab combines behavioural experiments and computational modelling with electrochemical (dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline) and electrophysiological (spiking activity, LFPs) recordings from deep brain structures in awake neurosurgical patients. In addition, the DCN lab uses functional neuroimaging (fMRI/EEG) and online data collection in healthy participants. The successful applicant will support the lab’s research. This work will include the development of tasks that probe decision-making, learning and mental representations; liaison with neurosurgeons, neurologists and other clinical staff; data collection in the operating room, typically in the context of the surgical implantation of deep brain stimulating (DBS) electrodes for the treatment of movement disorders (e.g., Parkinson’s disease, essential tremor); behavioural testing and neuroimaging (e.g., EEG) in healthy volunteers; statistical analysis and modelling of data; and the preparation of publications, conference presentations and other research outputs. The nature of the work means that travel and flexibility with working hours may occasionally be required (e.g., early start on surgery days). The position is suited for students who are pursuing a master’s degree in a relevant subject area, such as cognitive science, neuroscience or psychology. The applicant must have experience with programming, data analysis and visualisation using Matlab, R and/or Python. Experience with task development, the acquisition and analysis of behavioural/neural data and computational modelling is strongly desired. Note that this is not a remote position: the successful applicant will be expected to attend the lab in person to conduct their tasks and to attend lab events. Your job responsibilities As a Student Assistant, your main tasks will be: To support data collection To support data analysis To support manuscript preparation To collaborate with lab members and other project participants as required You will report to Associate Professor Dan Bang. Your qualifications You must be an active student and provide proof of enrolment at an institution of higher education. Specific qualifications for the position are: Master’s student in a relevant subject area such as cognitive science, neuroscience or psychology Proficiency in programming, data analysis and visualisation using Matlab, R and/or Python Excellent communication skills in English and Danish, both written and oral Willingness to travel and flexibility with work scheduling Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Dan Bang, [email protected] . Your place of work will be the Center of Functionally Integrative Neuroscience, Department of Clinical Medicine, which is located at (1) Aarhus University, Universitetsbyen 3, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark and (2) Aarhus University Hospital, Palle Juul-Jensens Boulevard 99, Entrance J, 8200 Aarhus N, Denmark. Terms of employment Terms of employment and salary are in accordance with the Circular on the collective agreement for student teachers at universities, etc. Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Indication of education (a copy of the diplomas should be uploaded) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the recruitment system We refer to the faculty’s Guideline for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Dan Bang Associate Professor, Department of Clinical Medicine - Center of Functionally Integrative Neuroscience [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4520938529 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday 29 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/student-assistant-for-the-international-recruitment-team": {
      "title": "Student Assistant for the International Recruitment team",
      "description": "Student Assistant for the International Recruitment team - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact AU Student Administration and Services - Recruitment and Guidance Student Assistant for the International Recruitment team Deadline 1 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Dec AU Student Administration and Services - Recruitment and Guidance Fredrik Nielsens Vej 5 8000 Aarhus C Part-time position 10 hours per week ID: 171038 Apply The AU International Student Recruitment team is looking for a new student assistant to help with the AU Student Ambassador Network and other tasks. The student assistant will be part of the International Recruitment team in the Recruitment and Guidance department. The team consists of 5 full-time staff members and around six student assistants. Primary tasks You will play an important role in the day-to-day coordination of the AU Student Ambassador Network. The Student Ambassador Network brings together international students at Aarhus University who help prospective students learn more about studying at Aarhus University and student life in Denmark. Your tasks will include: Staying in regular contact with our Student Ambassadors and following up on their activities on Unibuddy Helping recruit and onboard new Student Ambassadors Helping plan and carry out events for the Student Ambassador Network Helping arrange Instagram takeovers Supporting international student events and activities across AU, such as Intro/ Welcome Days, webinars and campus tours Assisting with practical and administrative tasks related to international student activities and communication The tasks vary throughout the year, so you should enjoy a role that combines communication, hands-on event support, coordination and administration! Expected qualifications We expect that you: Have experience using Excel, Forms, PowerPoint, Teams, and other digital tools Have excellent written and spoken English Are organized and detail-oriented, and comfortable keeping track of several ongoing tasks Can communicate confidently by email and through online platforms Are responsible, reliable and able to take initiative Are approachable and comfortable communicating with students from different countries and cultural backgrounds Are flexible, as some activities and events may take place outside normal office hours It is an advantage if you: Have previously participated in the AU Student Ambassador Network Have experience organizing events or student-led initiatives Have been involved in student organizations or volunteer networks As our Student Assistant, you will get the opportunity to: Work independently while being part of a collaborative team Work closely with international students from across AU and build a broad network Gain hands-on experience with event planning and coordination Work with Unibuddy and other digital platforms used in international student recruitment Develop your communication, administrative, organizational and intercultural skills Location Your place of work will be the Recruitment and Guidance Office at Frederik Nielsens Vej 5, 8000 Aarhus C Pay The position is paid according to the collective agreement between the Danish Ministry of Finance and HK ( read the collective agreement here ). Who can apply The AU International Office encourages students who have at least one year left of their studies in Denmark to apply for the position. Please note that you need to be a full-time student at a higher learning institution in order to apply. If you have any questions - please contact: Gitte Bindzus Foldager, International Communications and Student Recruitment Manager Email: [email protected] Phone no. 23 31 63 55 Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Gitte Bindzus Foldager Communications and Recruitment Manager, AU Student Administration and Services - Recruitment and Guidance +4523316355 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? AU HR - Employment Law +4529454108 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday  1 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/four-year-fixed-term-academic-employee-position-in-bioacoustics-and-signal-processing": {
      "title": "Four-year fixed term academic employee position in bioacoustics and signal processing",
      "description": "Four-year fixed term academic employee position in bioacoustics and signal processing - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology Four-year fixed term academic employee position in bioacoustics and signal processing Deadline 2 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Mar Department of Biology Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2031 ID: 171010 Apply The Marine Bioacoustics lab at the Section for Zoophysiology at the Department of Biology, Aarhus University, is seeking a skilled and motivated bioacoustician to spearhead the collection and high-end analysis of calibrated acoustic data from monitoring programs, archival tags and animal experiments. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a four-year fixed term position funded by external grants starting 1 March 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description You will be responsible for planning, preparing and executing field work in Denmark and internationally using hydrophone and microphone arrays, DAS, on animal tags and passive acoustic monitoring devices. You will manage and maintain our recording equipment and undertake calibration and development of scientific equipment. You will set up and curate data bases and develop and use software to perform discrete time series analyses of large acoustic and sensor datasets. You will help supporting and training students and post docs in acoustic analyses and contribute to both specific and general courses as a co-teacher. You will take the lead on writing reports to funders and governments and contribute to peer-reviewed papers and grant applications with data, plots, analyses and writing. Your profile You must h ave a master degree in bioacoustics, engineering or physics with strong analytical skills in quantitative acoustics and signal processing. A PhD degree and relevant experience within the specified areas will be considered an advantage. You must have documented experience with programming in Matlab or Python or similar programming tools. At sea field experience and boat handling skills are an advantage and so is work experience with data bases and data curation. You must have strong English skills both orally and in writing, and you must thrive in an inclusive multi-cultural and diverse working environment. We strive to be best bioacoustics lab in the world, and we are therefore looking for a profile for this job who shares that ambition with the needed attention and care to get things right on time. Who we are The Marine Bioacoustics lab currently consists of 2 senior PI´s, 2 AC-taps, 9 post docs, 6 PhD students and several MSc and Bsc. It is part of the Section for Zoophysiology, Department of Biology, Aarhus University. www.marinebioacoustics.com What we offer A well-developed research infrastructure, acoustic laboratories and access to shared equipment and novel bioacoustic tools. An exciting interdisciplinary environment with many high level national and international collaborators. A research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research. A work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities. A workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is C.F. Mollers Alle 3, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Contact information For further information, please contact: Professor Peter T. Madsen, +45 5177 8771, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 2 November 2026. Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Peter Teglberg Madsen Professor, Department of Biology - Zoophysiology +4551778771 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522521469 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday  2 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-position-in-biology-cifar-center-of-excellence-aarhus-university": {
      "title": "Postdoc position in biology (CIFAR",
      "description": "Postdoc position in biology (CIFAR – Center of Excellence, Aarhus University) - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Aquatic Biology Postdoc position in biology (CIFAR – Center of Excellence, Aarhus University) Deadline 3 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Mar Department of Biology - Aquatic Biology Ole Worms Allé 1 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2029 ID: 170410 Apply Applications are invited for a two-year postdoctoral position within the Center for Ice-Free Arctic Research (CIFAR) and the Department of Biology at Aarhus University. Rapid warming is increasing freshwater inputs to the Arctic Ocean from melting glaciers, ice sheets, and altered precipiation patterns. At the same time, sea-ice cover is declining and the duration of the open-water season is increasing. These changes are fundamentally transforming the structure and functioning of the Arctic coastal ocean. CIFAR aims to understand how the complex interplay between ice melt, terrestrial runoff, and changing ocean conditions affects Arctic coastal ecosystems and their functioning across spatial and temporal scales. Greenland fjords are important sites of carbon cycling and can act as substantial sinks for atmospheric CO₂ during the productive summer season. Simultaneously, increasing freshwater inputs are altering the marine carbonate system and contributing to ocean acidification. Yet, our understanding of both CO₂ exchange and carbonate chemistry remains strongly biased toward the summer months, leaving major uncertainties about how these systems function across the full annual cycle. Ongoing changes in freshwater input, stratification and sea-ice cover may alter the magnitude and seasonality of air-sea CO₂ exchange. Longer ice-free periods provide a greater window for exchange with the atmosphere, while freshwater-driven changes in carbonate chemistry and stratification can further influence the capacity of surface waters to take up CO₂. However, we know comparatively little about what happens outside of summer, making it difficult to determine annual CO₂ uptake and predict how the carbon sink may respond to continued freshening and sea ice loss. The autumn transition is a particularly important, but poorly understood, period. As terrestrial runoff ceases and freshwater-driven stratification weakens, deeper mixing alters surface-water carbonate chemistry while waters remain exposed to the atmosphere before sea-ice formation. How important are these seasonal transitions for annual CO₂ uptake, how are they expected to change in the future, and how does their contribution compare with the strongly freshened summer period? This postdoctoral project will address these questions by investigating seasonal carbonate system dynamics and air-sea CO₂ exchange in Greenland fjords undergoing freshening. The successful candidate will combine field observations, experiments, and existing environmental datasets to determine how freshwater inputs, mixing, biological processes, and sea-ice cover regulate carbonate chemistry and CO₂ exchange across seasons. The postdoctoral researcher will: Participate in field campagins in Greenland, including collection of carbonate chemistry samples and measuring CO2 in the water column. Investigate seasonal changes in the marine carbonate system and controls on air-sea CO₂ exchange. Conduct experiments designed to disentangle the effects of freshwater input and other environmental drivers on carbonate chemistry. Integrate field observations, experimental results, and complementary datasets to quantify seasonal and annual CO₂ uptake in Greenland fjord systems. Assess how continued freshening and changing sea-ice conditions may alter the future role of Greenland fjords in the Arctic carbon cycle. The successful candidate will have opportunity to develop independent research questions within this framework and to integrate observations and experimental approaches across the wider CIFAR research program. Ultimately, the project will help determine how the capacity for CO₂ uptake acidiciation in Greenland's rapidly changing fjords will be affected in the future. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a two-year postdoctoral position (37 hours/week) from 1 March 2027. Qualifications The successful candidate should have: A PhD in chemical oceanography, marine science, or a related discipline. Excellent programming skills in R and Python or similar. Advanced statistical and data-analysis skills. The ability to work independently, develop research questions, and think creatively and critically. Strong written and oral communication skills in English. An ability to work effectively in an interdisciplinary and collaborative research environment. Experience with fieldwork in Arctic, polar, or other remote or extreme environments is highly desirable. Experience with public dissemintaiton and outreach of research results would also be an advantage. Place of work This position will be administered through Aarhus University. Place of employment is Center for Ice-free Arctic Research (CIFAR) at Aarhus University, Department of Biology, 1134, 220, Ole Worms Allé 1, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Do you have any questions? If you have any questions about the scientific aspects of the position, you are more than welcome to contact: professor Søren Rysgaard ( [email protected] ) Deadline All applications must be received no later than 3 November 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Søren Rysgaard Professor, Department of Biology - Aquatic Biology +4524643206 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday  3 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-for-distributed-coordination-in-swarm-robotics-for-underwater-anomaly-detection": {
      "title": "Postdoc for distributed coordination in swarm robotics for underwater anomaly detection",
      "description": "Postdoc for distributed coordination in swarm robotics for underwater anomaly detection - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Software Engineering & Computing systems - Faculty: Technical Sciences Postdoc for distributed coordination in swarm robotics for underwater anomaly detection Deadline 3 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 15 Jan Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Software Engineering & Computing systems Helsingforsgade 10 8200 Aarhus N Fixed term full-time position 15 Jan 2027 - 14 Apr 2028 ID: 170385 Apply Are you interested in distributed coordination and autonomous decision-making in robot swarms, and can you contribute to the development of the international ITEA 4 project ADVISOR – Cooperative Missions of Autonomous Vehicle Swarms for Surveillance Tasks? Then the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Aarhus University, Denmark, invites you to apply for a postdoc position. The position is fixed-term from 15 January, 2027 to 31 March, 2028. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a fixed-term position from 15 January, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter, to end on 31 March, 2028. Job description ADVISOR brings together industrial and academic partners from across Europe and Korea to enable cooperative missions of heterogeneous autonomous vehicles – underwater, surface and aerial – for surveillance tasks such as the inspection of offshore wind turbines and other offshore infrastructure. Aarhus University contributes decision-making components that coordinate individual unmanned underwater vehicles (UUVs) and entire swarms based on local information, for anomaly detection and infrastructure surveying in environments where communication is extremely limited. As a postdoc in the project, you will: develop and implement cooperative mission planning for underwater infrastructure surveying and anomaly detection, including approaches for self-adaptation and re-planning of missions at runtime design distributed coordination and decision-making mechanisms that allow swarms of underwater vehicles to act on local information under severely limited communication validate the coordination approaches through autonomous multi-robot surveying of offshore infrastructure in a laboratory environment potentially apply co-simulation and digital-twin frameworks to develop and evaluate multi-robot collaboration These activities will be carried out in close collaboration with the Danish industrial partner EIVA and the wider international ADVISOR consortium. Your profile Applicants should hold a PhD in computer science, computer engineering, robotics, electrical engineering or a related field. Furthermore, you should have: experience with multi-agent systems, swarm robotics or distributed coordination of autonomous agents experience with multi-robot or mission planning, autonomous decision-making or self-adaptive systems experience with robot simulation, co-simulation or digital twins is an advantage experience with underwater, marine or field robotics is an advantage a track record of publications in relevant international conferences and journals strong programming skills the ability to work independently as well as in an international, interdisciplinary team with industrial partners excellent written and spoken English Who we are The position is based at the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE), Aarhus University, which conducts research and teaching in areas including computer engineering, robotics, autonomous systems and digitalisation, in close collaboration with industry. You will join the research group of Lukas Esterle, which works on autonomous decision-making for collective behaviour in multi-robot systems, with particular strengths in autonomous coordination, multi-robot planning, simulation and digital twins. The work is connected to the Aarhus University DIGIT centre and the EDIH TechCircle. Read more at ece.au.dk . What we offer The department offers: a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. the opportunity to work in a large international research and innovation project with leading industrial and academic partners Place of work and area of employment The place of work is the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Aarhus University, Helsingforsgade 10, 8200 Aarhus N, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. Contact information For further information, please contact: Lukas Esterle, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Aarhus University, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 3 November, 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Lukas Esterle Associate Professor, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Software Engineering & Computing systems +4522464893 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday  3 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
