    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/assistant-professor-in-cern-related-physics-and-detector-development": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in CERN-Related Physics and Detector Development",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in CERN-Related Physics and Detector Development - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Physics and Astronomy Assistant Professor in CERN-Related Physics and Detector Development Deadline 6 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Feb Department of Physics and Astronomy Ny Munkegade 120 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Feb 2027 - 31 Jan 2029 ID: 170191 Apply The Department of Physics and Astronomy, Aarhus University, Denmark, invites applications for a 2-year Assistant Professor position, possibly extendable to 3 years, in experimental CERN-related physics. The Department aims to strengthen and further develop its research activities within experimental physics related to CERN. The Department has long-standing expertise in CERN-related physics, with research activities in anti-hydrogen physics, low-energy nuclear physics, and strong-field quantum electrodynamics. More recently, the Department has expanded its research portfolio to include relativistic heavy-ion physics. The new Assistant Professor will contribute to this effort by strengthening the Department’s activities in detector development for CERN experiments while creating synergies with the activities of the existing CERN-related physics research group. Expected start date and duration of employment The position is available from February 1st, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. This is a 2-year fixed-term position ending on January 31st, 2029, possibly extendable by 1 year. Job description The successful candidate is expected to establish and lead research activities in detector development for CERN-based experiments. Relevant research areas include high-energy nuclear physics (ALICE, e.g,. ITS3 or FoCal), including associated data analysis tools and readout electronics, low-energy nuclear physics at ISOLDE (e.g., the ISOLDE Decay Station, IDS), and the development of the Luminosity Calorimeter (LumiCal) for the Future Circular Collider (FCC). The position is funded under the national CERN lighthouse initiative to strengthen Denmark's engagement with CERN, https://phys.au.dk/dirac-center. Your profile The ideal candidate has strong backgrounds in particle and/or nuclear physics (experimental as well as theoret-ical), detector development and testing, electronics and data analysis techniques and good knowledge of accelerator physics. The successful candidate must as a minimum: •    Hold a PhD in Physics or a closely related field •    Have demonstrated the ability to conduct independent research at high international level •    Have documented experience in teaching and supervision at university level and an ambition to strengthen these competences e.g. through taking on the responsibility of a physics course at the de-partment and completing the university pedagogical programme •    Demonstrate strong potential to establish an independent research programme •    Demonstrate the ability to engage in local, national and international research collaborations •    Be able to communicate effectively in English, both orally and in writing Who we are The Department of Physics and Astronomy (IFA) is part of the Faculty of Natural Sciences at Aarhus University. We conduct excellent, internationally recognized research across a broad range of fields within physics and astronomy and provide research-based education at Bachelor, Master and PhD levels. The Department is home to a vibrant community of researchers, technical and administrative staff, PhD students, and undergraduate and graduate students who all contribute to a dynamic and inspiring environment. The Department conducts excellent research at the highest international level, with several research areas among the world elite. Our researchers participate in numerous international collaborations with leading universities, research institutions, and large-scale research facilities, including CERN. Read more about the Department of Physics and Astronomy at: https://phys.au.dk/en. What we offer The successful candidate will join an ambitious, international and collaborative research environment with strong support for both professional and personal development. The Department offers excellent opportunities to develop an independent research profile, establish new collaborations, and contribute to the education and su-pervision of students at all levels. We foster an inclusive working culture characterized by openness, mutual respect, and a healthy worklife balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Ny Munkegade 120, 8000 Aarhus C, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. Contact information For further information, please contact: Head of Department, Professor Ulrik Ingerslev Uggerhøj, phone +4523382373, email [email protected] . Deadline The application deadline is November 6th, 2026 Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities. Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Ulrik Ingerslev Uggerhøj Head of Department, Department of Physics and Astronomy +4523382373 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4593522463 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Friday  6 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
