    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-researcher-in-mass-spectrometry-and-proteomics-technology-development": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Mass Spectrometry and Proteomics Technology Development",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Mass Spectrometry and Proteomics Technology Development - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Protein Science Postdoctoral Researcher in Mass Spectrometry and Proteomics Technology Development Deadline 23 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 15 Jan Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Protein Science Universitetsbyen 81 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 15 Jan 2027 - 14 Jan 2029 ID: 171270 Apply Applications are invited for a two-year postdoctoral position in mass spectrometry and proteomics technology development at the Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics (MBG), Aarhus University, Denmark. The position is available in the Nielsen Laboratory, which focuses on developing advanced mass spectrometry-based technologies to understand regulatory protein states, post-translational modifications (PTMs), and cellular signalling. We are seeking an ambitious and technically skilled researcher with demonstrated experience in developing, establishing, or optimizing mass spectrometry-based proteomics methodologies. Candidates should have a strong background in at least one area of proteomics technology development, including LC-MS/MS method development, chromatographic optimization, sample preparation, PTM analysis, or related analytical technologies. The position offers an exciting opportunity to pursue innovative technology development while taking an active role in building and shaping a growing research group at Aarhus University. Expected start date and duration of employment The position is a two-year postdoctoral appointment with the possibility of extension. The expected start date is 15 January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description The successful candidate will join a growing research programme focused on developing advanced mass spectrometry-based proteomics technologies and applying these to fundamental questions in molecular and cellular biology. Our research integrates state-of-the-art mass spectrometry, quantitative proteomics, analytical method development, peptide and protein chemistry, and PTM biology to investigate how regulatory protein states control cellular function. A central objective of the position is to develop innovative analytical methodologies that expand the capabilities, sensitivity, robustness, and throughput of modern proteomics. Potential research directions include advanced LC-MS/MS acquisition strategies, quantitative PTM proteomics, chromatographic optimization, automated sample preparation, and novel approaches for studying protein regulation. The position provides considerable scientific freedom to develop and pursue new technological ideas, with opportunities to establish independent research directions and translate methodological innovations into high-impact scientific publications. As an early member of a newly established research group at Aarhus University, the successful candidate will also have a unique opportunity to actively contribute to the development of the laboratory's scientific activities, technological capabilities, and future research directions. Primary responsibilities Develop, establish, and optimize innovative mass spectrometry-based proteomics methodologies. Design and independently drive research projects with a strong emphasis on analytical innovation and technological advancement. Explore and implement new approaches to improve proteomics sensitivity, quantitative accuracy, reproducibility, and analytical throughput. Develop and apply advanced proteomics technologies to investigate PTMs and regulatory protein states. Contribute to establishing new experimental workflows and analytical capabilities within the laboratory. Collaborate with national and international research partners across proteomics, analytical chemistry, and molecular biology. Communicate research findings through publications in leading international scientific journals and presentations at scientific conferences. Contribute to the scientific development and continued expansion of the research group, including mentoring junior researchers and developing new collaborative projects. Your profile Applicants must hold a PhD in proteomics, analytical chemistry, biochemistry, molecular biology, biotechnology, or a closely related discipline. We are particularly interested in candidates who have demonstrated the ability to develop, establish, or substantially improve experimental and analytical methodologies, rather than solely applying established proteomics workflows. Applicants must demonstrate significant hands-on experience and scientific contributions in at least one of the following areas: Mass spectrometry method development: Establishment or optimization of advanced LC-MS/MS acquisition methodologies, including data-independent acquisition (DIA), fragmentation strategies, instrument parameter optimization, or related approaches for improving analytical performance on modern mass spectrometry platforms. Liquid chromatography and separation technologies: Development and optimization of chromatographic methodologies for proteomics, including LC gradient design, peptide separation, nanoLC or microflow LC, improved peak capacity, or approaches to enhance sensitivity, robustness, and throughput. Proteomics sample preparation and workflow development: Development or substantial optimization of protein and peptide sample preparation methodologies, including automated or high-throughput workflows, digestion strategies, sample clean-up, enrichment techniques, or quantitative proteomics applications. Post-translational modification proteomics: Development and optimization of methodologies for PTM enrichment, characterization, or quantitative analysis, including phosphorylation, ADP-ribosylation, ubiquitination, or other regulatory protein modifications. Peptide and protein chemistry: Development of innovative approaches involving peptide or protein chemistry, chemical modification, affinity enrichment, selective capture, or related analytical strategies relevant to mass spectrometry-based proteomics. Innovative proteomics technologies: Development of other advanced analytical approaches, such as low-input or single-cell proteomics, alternative fragmentation techniques, novel sample processing technologies, or integrated analytical workflows. Expertise in one of these areas is sufficient. Applicants are not expected to possess experience across all the listed technologies. We particularly welcome researchers with a strong technical specialization who are motivated to expand their expertise into complementary areas of proteomics technology development. Demonstrated scientific achievements A central selection criterion will be the applicant's demonstrated contribution to methodological development and technological innovation. Applicants are expected to document relevant expertise through at least one first-author research publication in which they have made a substantial contribution to the development, establishment, or optimization of an analytical method or experimental technology. In their application, candidates should identify the publication(s) most relevant to this requirement and briefly describe their personal contributions, the technological challenge addressed, and the methodological advances achieved. Additional qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas will be considered an advantage but is not required: Proteomics data processing, bioinformatics, quantitative data analysis, or systems biology. Advanced computational analysis of LC-MS/MS data, including DIA data processing and statistical evaluation. Mammalian cell culture, including experimental perturbations and cellular model systems. Handling and processing of biological tissues, clinical samples, or other complex biological materials. Development or implementation of laboratory automation and high-throughput experimental workflows. Protein biochemistry, molecular biology, or functional studies of protein regulation. Programming, scripting, or computational workflow development, including R or Python. Experience working across interdisciplinary projects involving analytical technology development and biological applications. Personal qualifications We are looking for a researcher who: Is scientifically ambitious, highly motivated, and driven by developing innovative solutions to challenging analytical problems. Demonstrates creativity, initiative, and a strong ability to translate scientific ideas into experimental advances. Takes ownership of research projects and has the determination to drive them from initial concept to publication. Enjoys solving technical challenges and continuously improving experimental methodologies. Thrives in a dynamic research environment where new ideas, technologies, and scientific directions are actively developed. Works independently while contributing positively to a collaborative and supportive research environment. Has strong scientific communication skills and an interest in developing an independent research profile. Who we are The position will be based in the Nielsen Laboratory at the Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics (MBG), Aarhus University. Our research focuses on understanding how dynamic post-translational modification networks regulate protein function and cellular behaviour. We develop and apply innovative mass spectrometry-based technologies to characterize regulatory protein states and uncover fundamental mechanisms controlling cellular function in health and disease. The laboratory builds on more than 15 years of internationally recognized research in regulatory proteomics, ADP-ribosylation biology, and mass spectrometry-based technology development. We are currently establishing and expanding our research activities at Aarhus University, combining technological innovation with fundamental biological discovery. Joining the laboratory at this stage provides a particularly attractive opportunity for a motivated researcher to help build a research environment, influence its technological development, establish new analytical capabilities, and contribute to defining future research priorities. The group benefits from strong international scientific connections and an interdisciplinary research environment spanning molecular biology, genome stability, structural biology, enzymology, and advanced proteomics. MBG provides an outstanding interdisciplinary research environment with state-of-the-art infrastructure and a strong commitment to scientific excellence. More information: https://mbg.au.dk What we offer We offer: A unique opportunity to take an active role in establishing and shaping a growing proteomics research group at Aarhus University. Considerable scientific freedom to develop innovative technologies and pursue independent research ideas. Access to advanced mass spectrometry and proteomics research infrastructure. Opportunities to pioneer analytical workflows and establish technological capabilities with broad scientific applications. A research environment where technological innovation, scientific ambition, and independent initiative are strongly encouraged. Strong opportunities for high-impact scientific publications, international collaborations, and conference participation. Support for career development, scientific networking, and applications for independent research funding. Close interaction with national and international researchers working at the forefront of proteomics, PTM biology, and molecular biology. A collaborative and supportive environment that values scientific creativity, technical excellence, and intellectual curiosity. Place of work and area of employment Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics, Aarhus University, Universitetsbyen 81, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Contact information For further information, please contact: Professor Michael L. Nielsen Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics Aarhus University Email: [email protected] Deadline Applications must be received no later than 23 November 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Michael Lund Nielsen Professor, Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Protein Science +4520931267 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 23 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/tenure-track-position-as-assistant-professor-in-human-security-department-of-anthropology-au": {
      "title": "Tenure-track position as Assistant Professor in Human Security, Department of Anthropology, AU",
      "description": "Tenure-track position as Assistant Professor in Human Security, Department of Anthropology, AU - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology - Faculty: Arts Tenure-track position as Assistant Professor in Human Security, Department of Anthropology, AU Deadline 23 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Sep School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology Moesgård Allé 20 8270 Højbjerg Fixed term full-time position 1 Sep 2027 - 31 Aug 2032 ID: 171752 Apply The Department of Anthropology, School of Culture and Society, invites applications for a tenure track position as assistant professor in Human Security. The position will commence on 1 September 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. An assistant professorship is a full-time, training position that involves research and teaching at the BA, MA and PhD level and includes an obligatory course in university teaching for assistant professors. The tenure-track position begins with a full-time, fixed-term three-year appointment as assistant professor with a view to permanent employment as associate professor. Tenure as an associate professor is subject to necessary funding and conditional upon the assistant professor being assessed as qualified for the position of associate professor before the end of the third year of employment at the latest or, if the tenure-track candidate requests, before the end of the third year. The assessment is based on the assistant professor’s fulfilment of the qualification requirements that apply to associate professorships and the criteria stated in this advertisement. If the assessment is positive, the candidate will be offered a tenured position as an associate professor at Aarhus University. Place of employment : Aarhus University, Department of Anthropology, Moesgård Alle 20, 8270 Højbjerg, Denmark. Don’t feel you meet every single requirement? Studies have shown that some groups of people are less likely to apply to jobs unless they meet every single qualification. We are dedicated to building a diverse, inclusive and authentic workplace, so if you’re excited about the position but your past experience doesn’t align perfectly, we encourage you to get in touch or to just apply. You may be just the right candidate. In its pursuit of academic excellence, The Faculty of Arts is committed to creating an inclusive working environment and therefore encourages all qualified candidates to apply, regardless of personal background, gender, sexual orientation, age, disability, ethnicity etc. The Position The position is a combined teaching and research position. For this position, we are seeking an innovative and dedicated scholar who will contribute to Aarhus University’s core activities in research, education, talent development, and knowledge exchange. The successful applicant will also be expected to coordinate the interdisciplinary master's degree program in Human Security and contribute to our general anthropology programs anchored at the Department of Anthropology. Research The Department of Anthropology emphasizes high-quality research and attaches great importance to interdisciplinary collaboration, participation in collective research projects, and the ability to attract external research funding. The successful applicant is expected to have a background in the social sciences or humanities and a strong research profile within human security. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research addresses themes such as crisis, security, pandemics, climate change, conflict, violence, post-conflict recovery, displacement, security governance, governmentality, and nation-building. The position is not restricted to a particular geographical region. However, applicants with documented research experience employing ethnographic and qualitative approaches, including extensive field-based research, will be preferred. Education & talent development The Department of Anthropology is home to three education programs: A bachelor and master program in anthropology, including a general track and a track in visual anthropology, and an international master program in Human Security. The assistant professor will be expected to teach and supervise students across these programs, at both BA and MSc level. The successful applicant will teach and supervise students in English at all levels (BA, MA, and PhD) and contribute to curriculum development across our educational programs. They will be responsible for developing and coordinating our MA program in Human Security and for teaching core courses. We therefore particularly value candidates who can teach courses related to human security, conflict and violence, humanitarian crises, peace and conflict studies, post-conflict reconstruction, migration and displacement, and ethnographic research methods. The candidate is also expected to engage in innovative teaching practices and collaborative approaches to student learning. We emphasise the importance of maintaining a dedicated and respectful relationship between staff and students, so applicants should be able to demonstrate and exemplify their commitment to participatory teaching initiatives and student involvement as part of their teaching portfolio. The successful candidate will contribute actively to talent development within the field of human security. This includes supervising MA students and PhD candidates and developing doctoral-level training in areas related to human security, conflict studies, violence, and ethnographic research methodologies. Knowledge Exchange The successful applicant will be expected to engage actively in knowledge exchange with governmental, non-governmental, humanitarian, and civil society organisations. Candidates should demonstrate an ability to communicate research findings to broader audiences and contribute to public debates on issues such as conflict, violence, humanitarian crises, post-conflict recovery, and human security. Experience collaborating with practitioners, policy actors, international organisations, or community-based partners working in conflict-affected settings is highly desirable. Qualifications Applicants must hold a PhD degree or equivalent qualifications in a field directly relevant to Human Security and have documented teaching qualifications relevant to the department’s degree programs. An internationally recognized publication profile in areas relevant to human security. Applicants must submit a full publication list and a maximum of three publications. Substantial ethnographic and/or field-based research experience, preferably conducted in conflict-affected, crisis, or post-conflict settings. Teaching experience in areas relevant to human security and demonstrated excellence in teaching in English. Experience with interdisciplinary collaboration. Experience and interest in attracting external research funding. Experience supervising student projects and interest in contributing to researcher talent development. Experience collaborating with non-academic stakeholders, including governmental agencies, NGOs, humanitarian organisations, or local communities. Research qualifications will be assessed in relation to the period of active research, the originality and quality of academic contributions, and publications in internationally recognised peer-reviewed journals and leading academic presses. If the successful applicant is not fluent in Danish, he or she will be expected to learn Danish within approximately two years. Only submitted publications will be assessed; a list of publications is not sufficient. As a result, applications without submitted publications will not be assessed. References or recommendations should not be included with the application. Applicants who are selected for a job interview may be asked to provide professional references For further information about the content of the position, please contact Head of Department Nina Holm Vohnsen [email protected] . For further information about the application procedure, please contact [email protected] The work environment The Department of Anthropology offers a welcoming atmosphere, a caring and including collegial environment, a superb student population, explorative and internationally orientated research and teaching environments, and good possibilities for career development, project funding, and administrative support at the school and the faculty levels. The Department of Anthropology is one of Europe's largest and most vibrant teaching and research environments within its field. Scientific staff currently consists of 32 associate and full professors, 12 assistant professors and postdocs, and 28 PhD-fellows, we are also the temporary home of a number of visiting and affiliated researchers. We are an open and dynamic community of staff and students that explores the fascinating diversity of the worlds that humans inhabit and addresses the burning issues of our time, developing new theoretical and methodological approaches along the way in order to understand and tackle them. We have broad collaborations with various disciplines, including philosophy and history of ideas, biology, and medicine and host numerous externally funded research projects. Our research is critically engaged attending to contemporary issues on a local and global scale while seeking to push the boundaries of the state-of-the-art within our field. See a more detailed description of our research activities here: Research . The department is located at the Moesgaard Campus in the beautiful woodlands south of the city of Aarhus, adjacent to Moesgaard Museum . As a collegial environment, we emphasize the importance of mutual support and non-hierarchical interaction among all academic and non-academic staff. We are deeply passionate about our educational programs, and we are excited about our students, which is easy because they are brilliant, curious, and brave. We care about each other. Applicants are expected to contribute to the department’s academic and social environment, and the person appointed will therefore be expected to be present at the department a minimum of three days a week, in accordance with school policy. The School of Culture and Society The School of Culture and Society is a vibrant research community dedicated to understanding the cultural and social conditions that shape human life. Our work examines how people interact with their environments, from prehistory to the present, and across both local contexts and global dynamics. Through cross-sector and cross-disciplinary partnerships, we aim to drive research innovation and contribute to address the major questions facing society. In both research and education, we are committed to bringing the humanities into active dialogue with partners beyond the university. The School is committed to: Fostering an international environment characterized by strong academic and interdisciplinary collaboration. Embracing an innovative and experimental approach to research, teaching, and communication. Cultivating a school that both generates knowledge and educates students for the present and future. Advancing a broad engagement with digital humanities. Upholding a strong commitment to both local and global societal challenges. Creating an inclusive, diverse, and sustainable workplace and organization. Read more about the school here School of Culture and Society - Aarhus University International applicants International applicants are encouraged to check Family and work-life balance in Denmark (au.dk) and Attractive working conditions (au.dk) for further information about the benefits of working at Aarhus University and in Denmark, including healthcare, paid holidays and, if relevant, maternity/paternity leave, childcare and schooling. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including a Relocation and onboarding services for international AU staff and career counselling for expat partners. For information about taxation, see Taxation aspects of international researchers’ employment by AU Qualification requirements Applicants should hold a PhD or equivalent academic qualifications. Formalities Faculty of Arts refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities (the Appointment Order) . Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Further information on the application and supplementary materials may be found in Application Guidelines . The application must outline the applicant's motivation for applying for the position, attaching a curriculum vitae, a teaching portfolio, a complete list of published works, a research plan, copies of degree certificates and examples of academic production ( mandatory, but no more than two examples ). Please upload this material electronically along with your application. Please note that although the application process can be completed in the Aarhus University system without up-loading publications or a teaching portfolio, applications that do not include uploaded publications as well as a teaching portfolio (or equivalent documentation of teaching experience and qualifications) will not be considered. If nothing else is noted, applications must be submitted in English. Application deadline is at 11.59 pm Danish time (same as Central European Time) on the deadline day. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Shortlists may be prepared with the candidates that have been selected for a detailed academic assessment. A committee set up by the head of school is responsible for selecting the most qualified candidates. See this link for further information about shortlisting at the Faculty of Arts: shortlisting Terms of employment Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Finance and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Salary and terms of employment are governed by the Cirkulære om overenskomst for akademikere i staten (in Danish). You will be employed as Assistant Professor at base pay level 4–8 with additional supplements in accordance with the Cirkulære om protokollat om visse ansættelsesvilkår for videnskabeligt personale ved universiteter (in Danish). The job content and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of  Academic Staff at Universities . About Arts The Faculty of Arts is one of Northern Europe’s most significant faculties for research and higher education in the humanities, theology and education. Within this broad academic scope, the faculty comprises approximately 700 researchers, 200 PhD students, 9,000 Bachelor’s and Master’s degree students, and 1,500 further and continuing education students. We believe that the best ideas arise when different perspectives meet – and that research, teaching and innovation are best developed in dialogue with the surrounding society. You can find more information here: Faculty of Arts | Aarhus University The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website . Questions about the position\n                            ? Nina Holm Vohnsen Associate Professor, Head of Department, School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology +4526838862 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - Arts HR +4523382165 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 23 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/part-time-25-professor-in-medical-anthropology": {
      "title": "Part-time (25 %) Professor in Medical Anthropology",
      "description": "Part-time (25 %) Professor in Medical Anthropology - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology - Faculty: Arts Part-time (25 %) Professor in Medical Anthropology Deadline 23 Nov 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Apr School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology Moesgård Allé 20 8270 Højbjerg Part-time position 9.25 hours per week ID: 171755 Apply The School of Culture and Society, Department of Global Studies, at Aarhus University invites applications for a permanent position as a part-time (25%) full professor in medical anthropology. The position is available from 1st April 20207. Place of employment: Department of Anthropology, Moesgaard Allé 20, 8270 Højbjerg, Denmark. The Faculty of Arts is committed to creating an inclusive working environment and therefore encourages all qualified candidates to apply, regardless of personal background, gender, sexual orientation, age, disability, ethnicity etc. Position The part-time (25%) full professorship is offered with a view to attracting well qualified applicants with an extensive and documented extensive research experience in innovative and internationally recognized research in the area of medical anthropology. The selected applicant will be expected to contribute to core activities at the Department of Anthropology, the School of Culture and Society, Aarhus University in general and to expand the research activities, outreach and output in the area of health research in particular. Apart from research and out research, the professor will also be expected to take an active role in supervising and supporting early career scholars, and in teaching and developing the department’s educational programs within anthropology. Research The selected applicant will be expected to provide academic leadership in the development of interdisciplinary research programs in the area of health and is expected to develop new research projects with internal and external partners, to raise external research funding, and to take part in the daily activities of the department. They will be expected to take an interest in brokering and entering inter- or transdisciplinary research cooperations both within and outside of the School of Culture and Society at the Faculty of Arts and AU in general. The applicant’s research activities will be evaluated in relation to research time. We therefore encourage applicants to specify any periods of research leave they may have taken (for instance maternity/paternity leave) so that we can take these periods into consideration when evaluating research productivity . The Department of Anthropology The Department of Anthropology offers a welcoming atmosphere, a close-knit and collegial environment, a superb student population, explorative and internationally orientated research and teaching environments, and good possibilities for career development, project funding, and administrative support at the school and the faculty levels. The Department of Anthropology is one of Europe's largest and most vibrant teaching and research environments within its field. Scientific staff currently consists of 27 associate and full professors, 13 assistant professors and postdocs, and 28 PhD-fellows, we are also the temporary home of a number of visiting and affiliated researchers. We are an open and dynamic community of staff and students that explores the fascinating diversity of the worlds that humans inhabit and addresses the burning issues of our time, developing new theoretical and methodological approaches along the way in order to understand and tackle them. We have broad collaborations with various disciplines, including philosophy and history of ideas, biology, and medicine and host numerous externally funded research projects. Our research is critically engaged attending to contemporary issues on a local and global scale while seeking to push the boundaries of the state-of-the-art within our field. See a more detailed description of our research activities here: Research . The department is located at the Moesgaard Campus in the beautiful woodlands south of the city of Aarhus, adjacent to Moesgaard Museum . The work environment As a collegial environment, we emphasize the importance of mutual support and non-hierarchical interaction among all academic and non-academic staff. We are deeply passionate about our educational programs, and we are excited about our students, which is easy because they are brilliant, curious, and brave. We care about each other. Applicants are expected to contribute to the department’s academic and social environment, and the person appointed will therefore be expected to be present at the department a minimum of three days a week, in accordance with school policy. Education The selected applicant will be required to teach and supervise at all levels of the department’s degree programs[BA, MA and PhD] and will be expected to have extensive teaching experience at university level. Furthermore, they will be expected to take a leading role in the teaching as well as the development of teaching didactics and to take responsibility for the further development of inter- or transdisciplinary educational programs, especially in collaboration with academic staff in the department. We emphasise the importance of maintaining a professional and respectful relationship between staff and students, so the ideal candidate should be able to demonstrate and exemplify their commitment to participatory teaching initiatives and student involvement as part of their teaching portfolio. Talent development and knowledge exchange The selected applicant will be expected to be able to identify the development potential of junior researchers, to contribute to the talent development and supervision of PhD students, and to design and teach PhD courses. Moreover, they will be expected to engage in knowledge exchange as mentioned in the strategy for the Faculty of Arts, for instance in research cooperation with private companies, government consultancy, cooperation with civil society actors or the public dissemination of knowledge. Qualifications Applicants should hold a PhD in anthropology or equivalent qualification, and research and teaching qualifications equivalent to those acquired for a professorship, according to the Faculty of Arts’ criteria . Applicants must be able to document: an original and relevant academic production at the highest international level significant scholarly contributions to theoretical and thematic developments within medical anthropology a demonstrated research leadership and international research cooperation evidence of excellence in teaching and supervision skills at BA, MA and PhD level, including evidence of contribution to teaching innovation. skills within research management and a commitment to researcher talent development as well as the development and teaching of PhD courses. experience of attracting and administering external research funding. Applicants will be asked to present their vision for future research and developments in the field of medical anthropology in Aarhus and beyond. Applicants must provide a research plan explaining how they will meet the expectations of the professorship. A maximum of five submitted publications should be uploaded with the application. A list of publications is not sufficient and applications without submitted publications will not be assessed. Only submitted publications will be assessed; a list of publications is not sufficient. Applications without submitted publications will not be assessed. Professional references or recommendations should not be included. Applicants who are selected for interview may be asked to provide professional references. Language and further information Applicants can teach and supervise in English at university level. If the selected applicant is not fluent in Danish, they will be expected to acquire the language within a period of approximately two years. In order to maintain and develop the department’s research environment, the person appointed is expected to be present at the department on a daily basis. Applications must be uploaded in English. For further information about the position and the department, please contact Head of School Marie Vejrup Nielsen [email protected] . For further information about the application procedure, please contact [email protected] . School of Culture and Society The School of Culture and Society is a vibrant research community engaged in understanding the cultural and social conditions that shape human life. Our work examines how people interact with their environments, from prehistory to the present and across both local and global contexts. Through cross-sector and cross-disciplinary partnerships, we aim to support research innovation and contribute to addressing the major questions facing society. In both research and education, we are committed to bringing the humanities into active dialogue with partners beyond the university. The School is committed to: Promoting an international environment characterized by active academic and interdisciplinary collaboration. Encouraging innovative and experimental approaches to research, teaching, and communication. Cultivating environments that generate new knowledge and educate students for the future. Advancing broad engagement with digital humanities. Upholding a deep commitment to addressing both local and global societal challenges. Creating an inclusive, diverse, and sustainable workplace and organization. Read more about the school here School of Culture and Society - Aarhus University International applicants International applicants are encouraged to check Family and work-life balance in Denmark (au.dk) and Attractive working conditions (au.dk) for further information about the benefits of working at Aarhus University and in Denmark, including healthcare, paid holidays and, if relevant, maternity/paternity leave, childcare and schooling. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including a Relocation and onboarding services for international AU staff and career counselling for expat partners. For information about taxation, see Taxation aspects of international researchers’ employment by AU . Qualification requirements Applicants should hold a PhD or equivalent academic qualifications. Formalities Faculty of Arts refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities (the Appointment Order) . Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Finance and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Further information on the application and supplementary materials may be found in Application Guidelines . The application must outline the applicant's motivation for applying for the position, attaching a curriculum vitae, a teaching portfolio, a complete list of published works, research plan, copies of degree certificates and no more than five examples of academic production (mandatory) . Please upload this material electronically along with your application. Please note that although the application process can be completed in the Aarhus University system without up-loading publications or a teaching portfolio, applications that do not include uploaded publications as well as a teaching portfolio (or equivalent documentation of teaching experience and qualifications) will not be considered. If nothing else is noted, applications must be submitted in English. Application deadline is at 11.59 pm Danish time (same as Central European Time) on the deadline day. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Shortlists may be prepared with the candidates that have been selected for a detailed academic assessment. A committee set up by the head of school is responsible for selecting the most qualified candidates. See this link for further information about shortlisting at the Faculty of Arts: Shortlisting About Arts The Faculty of Arts is one of Northern Europe’s most significant faculties for research and higher education in the humanities, theology and education. Within this broad academic scope, the faculty comprises approximately 700 researchers, 200 PhD students, 9,000 Bachelor’s and Master’s degree students, and 1,500 further and continuing education students. We believe that the best ideas arise when different perspectives meet – and that research, teaching and innovation are best developed in dialogue with the surrounding society. You can find more information here: Faculty of Arts | Aarhus Universitet The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Marie Vejrup Nielsen Head of School, School of Culture and Society - Culture and Society, School secretariat +4593508852 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - Arts HR +4523382165 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 23 Nov 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/assistant-professor-tenure-track-associate-professor-in-structural-reliability-risk-and-resilience": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (tenure track)/Associate Professor in Structural Reliability, Risk and Resilience - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Structural Engineering - Faculty: Technical Sciences Assistant Professor (tenure track)/Associate Professor in Structural Reliability, Risk and Resilience Deadline 14 Dec 23:59 CET Expected start 1 Aug Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Structural Engineering Inge Lehmanns Gade 10 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 171364 Apply Are you an expert in the field of structural reliability, risk and resilience? Do you master uncertainty quantification and probabilistic modelling for analysing the response of structures? Are you motivated to create societal impact with your research? Then the Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering of Aarhus University invites you to apply for this position. Expected start date and duration of employment The expected starting date is no later than August 1 2027. Job description This position is part of the section for Structural Engineering. Your tasks will be: Teaching/upgrading existing courses related to Structural Engineering including Risk/Resilience/Reliability at both B.Sc. and M.Sc. level in Civil and Architectural Engineering programmes. Supervising students at different levels (e.g., multidisciplinary projects, master theses, doctoral theses). Conducting research through externally sponsored grants at national and international levels. Establishing relevant collaborations with the Danish industry. Contributing to the operation of the department. Your profile We are looking for a candidate that can enhance the department’s competencies in structural reliability, risk and resilience. The candidate shall hold: Engineering background (B.Sc. and M.Sc.). Doctoral degree in a relevant field of research. High-level proficiency in English. The following list of qualifications and experiences are a requirement: Risk, reliability and resilience analysis of civil engineering structures and infrastructures. Probabilistic modelling including stochastic process modelling, numerical methods for uncertainty quantification and rare event estimation. Scientific computing, possibly with different programming languages (e.g., Python, Julia, C#, MATLAB). Teaching and developing courses related to structural engineering. Supervision of undergraduate and graduate students (B.Sc., M.Sc., and Ph.D. theses). Experience with risk, reliability and resilience analysis of networks of infrastructures is particularly welcomed. Candidates must demonstrate potential for outstanding research and teaching. The selected candidate shall demonstrate: Some years of post-graduate research and publication experience are required. A pre-existing and growing international research network is expected. Prior collaboration with the Danish innovation ecosystem is desired, but not essential. Experience from successful external research collaborations with industry and academia, project management skills. Experience in the attraction of external funding is preferred. Research ability documented through rising publication and citation records evidenced by appropriate bibliometric parameters in reputable international peerreviewed journals. Well-defined research focus and sound strategy to develop a research group. Who we are The Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering at Aarhus University is a growing engineering environment with a clear vision for the future of the built environment. Established in 2021, the department today includes more than 100 researchers and educators and around 1.050 students. We conduct research and education within structural engineering, geotechnics, architectural engineering and design, sustainability, construction management, climate adaptation, indoor climate, building materials, and digital technologies. The construction sector is facing major challenges related to climate, resource consumption, and the way we design and build our cities and buildings. Through research, education, and collaboration with industry and public-sector partners, we contribute to developing technological solutions that support a more sustainable built environment. Our research and development activities are closely connected to the needs of industry and society, and many projects are carried out in collaboration with companies, public authorities, and other research environments. This ensures that the knowledge and technologies developed at the department are relevant in practice and create value beyond the university. Education is a central part of the department’s identity. We offer Bachelor’s, Master’s, and PhD programmes in engineering and continuously work to improve the connection between research, teaching, and practice. Across our programmes, students work with real-world challenges, current technologies, and sustainable solutions for the construction sector. What we offer We offer an innovative and collaborative teaching and research environment with close interaction between students, researchers, and industry partners. The department provides well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories, and access to shared technical facilities. We offer an interdisciplinary environment with national, international, and industrial collaborators. Our academic environment is built on collaboration, knowledge sharing, and professional dialogue. The workplace is characterised by professionalism, flexibility, and a healthy work–life balance. The department is located at Navitas on the waterfront in Aarhus, Denmark Place of work and area of employment The place of work is at Navitas, Inge Lehmanns Gade 10, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. The area of employment is Aarhus University with related departments. Contact information For further information, please contact: Associate Professor, Giuseppe Abbiati +45 93 52 17 51, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than December 14 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities. Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Giuseppe Abbiati Associate Professor, Head of Section, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Structural Engineering +4593521751 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520612514 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 14 Dec 2026 at 23:59 CET 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
